[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JSR223 Samplers and PostProcessors with Groovy in JMeter"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Apache JMeter provides robust support for scripting through JSR223 elements. Among the available scripting languages, Groovy is the recommended standard due to its high performance, seamless Java integration, and advanced caching capabilities."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Use Groovy for JMeter Scripting?"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Performance: Groovy scripts compile down to bytecode, running significantly faster than older alternatives like JavaScript or Beanshell."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Caching: JSR223 elements in JMeter support compiled script caching, preventing expensive recompilation on every iteration."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Java Compatibility: Groovy syntax is an extension of Java, making it easy to leverage standard Java libraries and JMeter API methods."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Using JSR223 Samplers"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"A JSR223 Sampler executes code at a specific point in your test plan, allowing you to generate custom requests, calculate complex data sets, or interface with external systems. Below is an example of a Groovy script that simulates a computational task and sets a JMeter variable."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"import java.util.UUID;

// Generate a unique transaction ID
String txnId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();

// Store the variable in JMeter context
vars.put("UNIQUE_TXN_ID", txnId);

// Log the generated ID for debugging
log.info("Generated Transaction ID: " + txnId);

// Set response data for the sampler
ResponseType = "Success";
SampleResult.setResponseData("Transaction ID generated: " + txnId, "UTF-8");
SampleResult.setSuccessful(true);"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Using JSR223 PostProcessors"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"PostProcessors execute immediately after a Sampler finishes. They are commonly used to extract values from responses, validate server output, or clean up variables. Here is how you can use a JSR223 PostProcessor to parse a JSON response and extract a dynamic token."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"import groovy.json.JsonSlurper;

// Retrieve the response body from the previous sampler
String responseJson = prev.getResponseDataAsString();

// Parse the JSON string using JsonSlurper
JsonSlurper slurper = new JsonSlurper();
def parsedData = slurper.parseText(responseJson);

// Extract a specific attribute, for example, an access token
String accessToken = parsedData.access_token;

// Save it to a JMeter variable for subsequent requests
if (accessToken != null) {
    vars.put("AUTH_TOKEN", accessToken);
    log.info("Extracted Access Token successfully.");
} else {
    log.error("Failed to extract access token from response.");
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for JSR223 Elements"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always check the "Cache compiled script" option to ensure optimal test execution speeds."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid using __groovy() function calls inside heavy loops or frequent timers; prefer dedicated JSR223 elements instead."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use the built-in 'log' object to write troubleshooting details to the jmeter.log file rather than relying on System.out.println."}]
